  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amphiliidae/phractura_brevicauda.htm",
      "html": "<ul><li>IUCN Red List: Least Concern (needs updating, IUCN 2010)</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amphiliidae/phractura_brevicauda.htm",
      "html": "<p>Africa: Lower Congo River. Type locality: tributary of the Lobi River, south-west of Efulen, and Kribi River, South Cameroon; Abanga River, Ogowe.</p><p>The species is widespread within the Central Africa assessment region.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amphiliidae/phractura_brevicauda.htm",
      "html": "<p>These catfish inhabit fast flowing waters and spend a lot of their time clinging to aquatic leaves. If you provide long stemmed plants, such as Vallisneria, you will see this habit.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amphiliidae/phractura_brevicauda.htm",
      "html": "<p>To keep this species and other members of this African hillstream family you will have to provide a well oxygenated planted aquarium and keep up your water changes, as they are not as hardy as their South American equivalents.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amphiliidae/phractura_brevicauda.htm",
      "html": "<p>Commonly known as 'African Whiptails', these catfish look at first glance superficially like South American whiptails such as Rineloricaria/Hemiloricaria. Dorsal spines (total): 0; anal spines: 0. Diagnosis: caudal peduncle relatively short, 3–4.2 times in SL and slender, length 8–16 times depth. Etymology: the specific name brevicauda means 'with short caudal'.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amphiliidae/phractura_brevicauda.htm",
      "html": "<p>Ferraris, C.J. Jr., 2007. Checklist of catfishes, recent and fossil (Osteichthyes: Siluriformes), and catalogue of siluriform primary types. Zootaxa 1418:1-628. Froese, R. and D. Pauly. Editors. 2021. FishBase. www.fishbase.org (08/2021). Moelants, T. 2010. Phractura brevicauda. The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species 2010. Skelton, P.H. and G.G. Teugels, 1986. Amphiliidae. p. 54-65. In J. Daget, J.-P. Gosse and D.F.E. Thys van den Audenaerde (eds.) Check-list of the freshwater fishes of Africa (CLOFFA). ISNB, Brussels; MRAC, Tervuren; and ORSTOM, Paris. Vol. 2.</p>"
    }
